\DOC SIMD_SIMPLIFY_TAC

\TYPE {SIMD_SIMPLIFY_TAC : thm list -> tactic}

\SYNOPSIS
{SIMD_SIMPLIFY_TAC} rewrites a goal's assumptions, normalising nested
word-level SIMD expressions (joins, subwords, shifts) into simpler equivalent
forms, leaving the conclusion unchanged.

\DESCRIBE
The argument {unfold_defs : thm list} supplies extra equational theorems
(typically definitions) that are expanded during the simplification, so that
terms built from the defined constants can be simplified too; it may be the
empty list. Assumptions whose bodies contain reducible word-level SIMD terms
are discharged and re-added in simplified form — often collapsing a large
{word_subword}/{word_join} nest into a compact expression, or evaluating a term
to a literal — which also moves them to the end of the assumption list;
assumptions already in normal form are left untouched, and in some applications
no assumption changes at all.
{
            A ?- t
   =================  SIMD_SIMPLIFY_TAC unfold_defs
           A' ?- t
}
\noindent where {A'} is {A} with zero or more assumptions replaced by
simplified versions.

\COMMENTS
All observed traces show the same shape of behaviour on goals arising from
machine-state reasoning, with large assumption lists and an unchanged
conclusion.

\ENDDOC
